    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok thanks. so the turbine part is basically just driven by the heat/pressure of the exhaust, right? and i've heard people talk about the specific sound a turbo makes when it's working hard, like that high pitched whistling or whining noise. is that just the air moving through the blades or is there something else happening mechanically?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, the turbine is driven by the heat and pressure of the exhaust gases. As exhaust gases pass through the turbine, their temperature and pressure cause it to spin. The high-pitched whistling or whining noise you mentioned, often referred to as the \"turbo whine,\" is actually the turbine blade lips or the compressor wheel's blades creating a resonance at high speed, often in the 10 kHz range. This occurs when air is accelerated and then swiftly decelerated through the compressor wheel's blades, causing a series of high-pitched, metallic tones.",
      "source": "target",
